CAT = "fitrkLoads"; SEG = "LOD"
# Road Traffic Act 729/2018 ss. 107-114, 150-152 and annex 7.2; EU speed limiter Directive 92/6/EEC.
Q = [
("Under the Road Traffic Act, how should a load be placed on a truck?",
 "Low and central", ["High at the front", "Mostly over the rear axle", "To one side for balance"],
 "The load should form as low and compact a unit as possible, with its centre of gravity low and close to the vehicle's longitudinal centre line."),
("What must a properly secured load do during braking, acceleration and cornering?",
 "Stay in place on the bed", ["Slide a little to absorb force", "Rest against the tail lift", "Lean onto the side walls"],
 "Section 109 requires the load to stay in place in all acceleration, braking and cornering, so it cannot affect the vehicle's safe handling."),
("Which is a recognised way of securing a load under Finnish law?",
 "Blocking against the headboard", ["Relying on the load's weight", "Driving more slowly", "Parking the heaviest item last"],
 "The Act lists blocking, lashing, locking and covering. Blocking against the headboard or other parts stops forward movement under braking."),
("When may friction between the load and the floor be counted towards load securing?",
 "Only if lashed down", ["Always, on any floor", "Never in any case", "Only on wet floors"],
 "Friction can be counted only when vertical movement of the load is prevented by lashing or similar, because a load that bounces loses its grip."),
("You carry gravel that could blow off on the road. What does the law require?",
 "Cover it with a tarpaulin", ["Drive under 60 km/h", "Wet it before leaving", "Load it below the sides only"],
 "A load must be covered if there is a risk that it will dust or spill onto the road in the wind of travel."),
("How far may a load reach out in front of a truck's foremost point?",
 "1 metre at most", ["0.5 metres at most", "2 metres at most", "3 metres at most"],
 "Within the vehicle's permitted length, a load may reach at most 1.00 metre in front and 2.00 metres behind the outermost part of the vehicle, unless a special transport permit says otherwise."),
("How far may a load normally extend behind the rearmost part of the vehicle?",
 "2 metres at most", ["1 metre at most", "3 metres at most", "4 metres at most"],
 "Section 107 allows a rear overhang of up to 2.00 metres, within the permitted length of the vehicle or combination."),
("A load sticks out 1.5 m behind your truck in daylight. How must you mark it?",
 "A red or red-yellow flag", ["A small white pennant", "Hazard lights on all day", "No marking at all"],
 "A load that reaches more than 1.00 metre beyond the rear must be marked at its end with a red or red-yellow flag at least 300 by 300 mm."),
("The same overhanging load is carried after dark. What must the rear end show?",
 "Red light and red reflector", ["White light and white reflector", "An orange flashing light", "Only the flag, as by day"],
 "In darkness or poor visibility, a load that needs marking shows a red light and a red reflector to the rear, and a white light and white reflector to the front."),
("A load is wider than the truck itself. What must you do about the mirrors?",
 "Adjust or add mirrors", ["Fold them in to save space", "Remove the passenger mirror", "Nothing, mirrors are optional"],
 "If a load exceeds the vehicle's width, the mirrors must be adjusted or extra mirrors fitted so the driver can see along the sides and behind."),
("Unless a special transport permit says otherwise, what is the tallest a vehicle with its load may be in Finland?",
 "4.40 metres", ["4.00 metres", "4.20 metres", "4.80 metres"],
 "The Road Traffic Act's annex sets the maximum height of a car and trailer at 4.40 metres; anything taller needs a special transport permit."),
("What is the maximum permitted width of a bus?",
 "2.55 metres", ["2.40 metres", "2.60 metres", "2.75 metres"],
 "The annex sets 2.55 metres for a bus. Most other trucks and trailers may be 2.60 metres wide."),
("Before a commercial trip, what must the driver make sure of about the load?",
 "It is loaded by the rules", ["Only that the doors are locked", "Only that the paperwork matches", "Nothing, the loader is responsible"],
 "Section 114 makes the driver check, before the journey, that the vehicle is loaded according to the rules, unless a sealed load or similar makes that unreasonable."),
("The load was sealed by the sender and you cannot inspect it. During the drive, what remains your duty?",
 "Check it has not shifted", ["Nothing at all after loading", "Break the seal to check", "Drive at 50 km/h only"],
 "The driver must take care during the trip that the load placement and securing stay as required, and stop to check if handling or noises suggest it has moved."),
("A warehouse worker loaded and strapped your trailer. Under section 114, who answers for the strapping being right?",
 "Whoever loaded it", ["Only the vehicle owner", "Only the receiver", "Only the police"],
 "The person who placed and secured the load, or gave instructions for it, is responsible for it being right, alongside the driver's own duty to check."),
("A centre-axle trailer is loaded so the drawbar lifts off the hitch. What is wrong?",
 "It needs nose weight", ["Nothing, that is ideal", "The tyres are too soft", "The trailer is too short"],
 "A centre-axle trailer must press down on the towing vehicle's coupling. The nose load may be at most 10 percent of the trailer's permitted axle mass or 1,000 kg, whichever is less."),
("What does a speed limiter do on a heavy truck?",
 "Caps speed at 90 km/h", ["Caps speed at 80 km/h", "Caps speed at 100 km/h", "Warns only when speeding"],
 "EU law requires speed limiters set at 90 km/h on trucks over 3.5 tonnes. The road speed limit for a truck is still 80 km/h unless lower."),
("At what speed is a bus's speed limiter set under EU rules?",
 "100 km/h", ["80 km/h", "90 km/h", "110 km/h"],
 "Buses and coaches carry speed limiters set at 100 km/h. A bus may only use that speed where approved for 100 km/h and with no standing passengers."),
("You overload a truck beyond the manufacturer's permitted mass. What is one result on the road?",
 "Braking distance grows", ["Fuel use falls", "Steering gets lighter in a good way", "Tyres grip better"],
 "Overloading lengthens stopping distances, overheats brakes and tyres and can make the vehicle unstable. No permitted mass set by the manufacturer may be exceeded."),
("What counts as a load under the Road Traffic Act?",
 "A demountable body", ["The cab's sleeping bunk", "The fifth wheel coupling", "The fuel in the tank"],
 "Section 107 says an exchangeable (demountable) body carried on the vehicle is treated as its load."),
("A tall, empty curtain-sided trailer meets a strong crosswind on an exposed bridge. What is the main risk?",
 "Being blown over", ["Overheating brakes", "Running out of air", "Losing tachograph data"],
 "High-sided vehicles, especially empty ones, catch the wind. Slow down, hold the wheel firmly and watch for warnings on bridges and open stretches."),
("You stack light pallets above heavy ones near the rear doors. What is the better way?",
 "Heavy low and forward", ["Light at the bottom", "Heavy items by the rear doors", "All items on one side"],
 "Heavy goods go low and against the headboard so the centre of gravity stays low and the axle loads stay within limits."),
("What can happen when a heavy load sits too far back on a rigid truck?",
 "A light steering axle", ["The engine overheats", "The brakes lock up at once", "The truck becomes wider"],
 "Too much weight behind the rear axle unloads the front axle, so the steering loses grip, especially on slippery roads."),
("You drive a milk tanker that has collected from only a few farms. What happens when you brake?",
 "A forward surge", ["Less weight on the front", "Shorter braking distance", "No difference at all"],
 "Liquid in a part-filled tank surges forward under braking and sideways in bends. Brake early and gently and take bends slowly."),
]
